think Chose the SPATIAL RELATION between two objects, crux-first: it is the most Lindy of the named options (a relational primitive a surface reads for years to arrange, order, and reason about a page of objects), and it lifts the family's SIGNATURE lesson -- box is a prefilter, the pixel map is the arbiter -- up one level to a PAIR, in BOTH directions at once.
think Designed object_relation(manifest, map, a, b) -> Relation: boxes_overlap + four STRICT directional flags (a left/right/above/below b, each true only when a's box sits wholly to that side) as the PREFILTER, and the EXACT nearest-pixel Chebyshev distance as the ARBITER. The arbiter gathers b's pixels into a list sized to b's area (bounded by label_max_pixels), then sweeps a's pixels once against it -- area_a * area_b distance reads, both under the pixel ceiling.
think The structural gem: two DIFFERENT labels are two distinct 8-connected components, so no pixel of one is 8-adjacent to a pixel of the other (that would have merged them), and the nearest Chebyshev distance is therefore ALWAYS at least 2 -- a proven invariant asserted as a postcondition, never a measured accident.
think Hand-verified both decisive fixtures before coding (measurement beats memory). Scene 1 (box over-claims NEARNESS): the hit_test L (box (1,1)-(5,5)) + block-in-concavity (box (3,2)-(4,3) wholly inside) -- boxes_overlap TRUE, yet nearest pixels 2 apart, no touch. Scene 2 (box over-claims FARNESS): two diagonal 2x2 blocks A(1,1)-(2,2), B(4,4)-(5,5) -- box-DISJOINT, a_left_of_b and a_above_b both TRUE, yet nearest pixels also exactly 2 apart, the tightest two distinct 8-components sit; a far dot C at (7,7) reads nearest 5, so the distance is a real graded measure that grows, not a touch flag.
obs GREEN on the FIRST full metal run, no reds. Every hand-verified value landed exactly (both nearest 2, the far 5, the mirror symmetric, refusals SameObject/BadObjectLabel). Avoided the brace-escape trap (no literal braces in the print) and split no compound asserts (used if(overlap){assert ...} rather than assert(a and b)) from the prior laps' lessons.
